#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933} :activity/id #uuid "d69714f2-bb12-5119-931a-cf96944e88c7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/id #uuid "06a51304-5013-5e6f-ab88-267fa31fcd9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172933 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173175 :tutorial/content "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2].$\n\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0,$ we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\n\n \nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2],$ these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad \nx \\in [0,2], \n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." :tutorial/id #uuid "9bd13796-1bf7-5a1b-a9f4-87567fd6998c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5305 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d490ab1-06f7-5e16-b643-b1b0d8fab4fe" :step/math-academy-id 33155 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172055 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171861 :question/id #uuid "1d32df15-74ee-5a3d-bc33-3d6452700e84" :question/math-academy-id "e-8879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ac4af78-af9b-5d48-a5d7-7d47ad92e348" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275400}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "9771f588-30ca-519b-9cd3-5da25e6f18e1" :step/math-academy-id 33156 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172056 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171862 :question/id #uuid "6ab25451-ad5c-5437-b4ba-046b0bd30fe6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8880"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415536}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "8409e7cf-c975-5a30-a407-4fc56e97763f" :step/math-academy-id 33157 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173186 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve $x=x(y)\\geq 0$ for $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y.$\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[ \n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve $z=z(y)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique.\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae882ec8-1ebd-5277-8224-b440b433c8ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5507 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the y-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "23271cc4-5b18-5ab7-87a0-420c4bf3d6fc" :step/math-academy-id 34312 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172132 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171712 :question/id #uuid "8ed57b1f-0082-5281-b061-ff2020ed7d66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048384}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415535}] :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2109942c-c8ff-56d5-8311-ec29a24f7390" :step/math-academy-id 44310 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173084 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173253 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution.\n\nSuppose that the curve [math]y=f(x)[/math] for [math]x \\in [a,b][/math] is rotated [math]2\\pi[/math] radians about the [math]x[/math]-axis. The corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad (x,\\theta) \\in D, \n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]D= \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: x \\in[a,b], \\: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi) \\big\\}.[/math] How can we find the area of this surface?\n\nFirst, we now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 1, \\:f'(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 0, \\: -f(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\\n0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta\n\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\bigg\\langle \\begin{vmatrix} f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ 0  & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta \\\\ 0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta  \\end{vmatrix} \\bigg\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x)\\cos^2\\theta+f(x)f'(x)\\sin^2\\theta, \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x), \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\:- f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nand\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|\n&= \\sqrt{\\big( f(x)f'(x) \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 \\big( \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\big) } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 }.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA \n&= \\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|  \\text{d}x\\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}\\theta \\: \\text{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\big[ \\theta \\big]_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\pi \\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}x.\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "79f42de8-4cc4-576a-b367-c08bd87f8bcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7638 :tutorial/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "8c37f4ed-7df7-5a6c-855e-263d81b3f5b0" :step/math-academy-id 44313 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172133 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171713 :question/id #uuid "a2ff0c76-0d26-51a2-a2fb-498ff5f85559" :question/math-academy-id "e-12365"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415537}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "3533d6b6-1b8e-5af8-b6c0-195bca89c86d" :step/math-academy-id 44314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173254 :tutorial/content "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x.$\n\nIn general, given a curve $y=y(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3148fb53-9ae7-5747-aa63-b79dd1bc224f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7648 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating y=y(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "93cfa70b-b133-5659-9bce-ab370759c49e" :step/math-academy-id 44408 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173255 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x(z),0,z)$ on the curve $x=x(z)\\geq 0$ for $z\\in [a,b]$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $z$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50473a5407143c7ec49ce8c192c256bbc184703010209767507e4b922e9f4c7b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: x(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nFinally, the rotation of a point $(0,y(z),z)$ on a curve $y=y(z)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $z$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/5f/5ff1574db4d105e7d69cc12ed609898d626b4977902f98dbcbcac6633ad56d2b.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle y(z) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(z) \\sin\\theta, \\: z \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e8a0947-f30a-5da6-9c3b-7b93bdba56bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7649 :tutorial/title "Formulas for Rotation About the z-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "6100252f-8102-5700-8f5a-11a2b77335a0" :step/math-academy-id 44409 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173257 :tutorial/content "Now consider the point $(x,0,z(x))$ that lies on a curve $z=z(x)$ in the $xz$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/74/74686e9e17ce056fea730b47bdf34ee582cc83920200f0345f75b90a6f8c9833.png)\n\nIn general, given a curve $z=z(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAgain, this parametrization is not unique. An alternative parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: z(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: z(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad z\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c06437e5-d847-5d03-aac0-497649901444" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7714 :tutorial/title "Parametrizing a Surface Generated by Rotating z=z(x) About the x-Axis"} :step/id #uuid "3100ec93-73c0-5fba-bebc-ee40c07ece7d" :step/math-academy-id 44739 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172934}}] :activity/title "Surfaces of Revolution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172185}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415539} :activity/id #uuid "b6ecbe9a-3094-59f8-872f-0af781e55451" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/id #uuid "06a51304-5013-5e6f-ab88-267fa31fcd9b" :topic/math-academy-id 3372} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0244fab1-dcd6-5c87-8769-552570471608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415511} {:answer/id #uuid "5ceeb26c-796b-593b-80a8-390a78233aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415518} {:answer/id #uuid "5f50a75f-3d11-54f7-b72f-ea3bd8cf01f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415519} {:answer/id #uuid "645d906f-848f-5e5e-92cb-479ed4f50490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{1-{z}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415521} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5125a1-4c8d-52e7-9aac-67e23cb420b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415511} :answer-field/id #uuid "638d8ce0-aca0-5ff6-85a7-9e3d7a370c18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a20cac79-e438-5720-941d-255bfdc76ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161037" :question/problem "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}$ by  $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$ What is the second  component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d3302b5f4ea4c0387d70ac8f78e03e486fff9579f1a6b5e5cec08a31cde76af3.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(z)=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$   \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,5],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"} :step/id #uuid "00cc2357-080e-51b5-a1e7-01af77a068b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74de9016-aec8-5c6f-9c78-665b929a97ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415522} {:answer/id #uuid "a106d988-56e8-55fb-9494-97df8b832cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415525} {:answer/id #uuid "c191f424-9120-5984-b367-462661abdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf41d2a-2802-588f-84c7-9bab46e26c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415529} {:answer/id #uuid "e38d5fae-3d16-5915-8cda-f00c09b669d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415527} :answer-field/id #uuid "247cda82-3b8b-57b4-bd85-1eaf3e646212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff19c12b-ba54-5c73-b456-a37b42a5dcc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-106277" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $1$st component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62191b79-d61b-528b-ad18-dd19267394a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415538}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fd857a-69bb-52bc-8fb2-b57c6fb65bf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+8{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371943} {:answer/id #uuid "1146da46-595d-55c2-874f-d7177d74a0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+8{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371944} {:answer/id #uuid "fc506864-2f86-51e9-b176-0329e981f791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371945} {:answer/id #uuid "82ddc8e9-2ce1-5932-875c-df7ef67f9b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371946} {:answer/id #uuid "67776efe-de42-57c3-8ff4-ba76605d7146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371947}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371947} :answer-field/id #uuid "83cff6c6-9176-54ce-9248-48c8a44d7c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5f49f4b-9d27-5249-8593-5beec661bda6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161096" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={z}^{4}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={z}^{4}+1\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [-1,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ and\n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({f}^{′}f)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(z)=1+{z}^{4},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(z)=4{z}^{3}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+(4{z}^{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1+{z}^{4})\\sqrt{1+16{z}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "883b0834-7022-5d35-a3fd-a77950a73c82"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "066318d7-b3c3-582b-8704-baaa946c3602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415512} {:answer/id #uuid "1a49bca9-5ff7-58a4-97d5-3b205c07eee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415514} {:answer/id #uuid "4ddf6f00-c938-50e9-9408-30046a58b1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} {:answer/id #uuid "6426d8d3-9d04-5e0f-a517-240093a9074a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415520} {:answer/id #uuid "8a136f45-dabb-5965-b74e-f1e60e2efbab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415516} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b1c789-64a3-55f5-9e7d-ac42dc183f0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b858f50b-1a14-576c-973e-c7199e8e8cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161090" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}).$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{y}{2})}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8f3fca3a-1b91-5ef9-86dc-c2de3c3b545f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a05c2e1-fb8c-50d3-b809-4010efe33716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(z-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415513} {:answer/id #uuid "41b7e536-caa1-5770-93ef-a558a35bf097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{(z-1)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415515} {:answer/id #uuid "5238fd8c-d50c-56c0-a63c-162bd3f52406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{z-1}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415517} {:answer/id #uuid "79004dcb-4e5d-5f5d-8fa0-3bf03f93e5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415523} {:answer/id #uuid "da7f796f-a386-5f76-a134-0786b69fca45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415523} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2a4e677-4714-5cf2-98da-8e4b0964eb3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0c98998f-cfca-568f-8a23-cab1aa107bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161060" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{z-1}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,10]$ is given above. What is the $3$rd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=\\sqrt{z-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,10].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,(z,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(z)=\\sqrt{z-1},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(z,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 10,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂z}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=\\sqrt{z-1},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z-1}}.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{z-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d593c770-7b73-5f58-8766-9937826ee041" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415541}}] :activity/title "Surfaces of Revolution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415510}]}
